# Are They Men Equal to Tigers?
Tamil title: புலி நிகர் மாத்தரே?7

“There lies the elephant, unable to withstand the spear's speed. He reclines upon its body as a mattress. A flock of hawks circles with beating wings, exulting over today's fine feast and spreading a canopy of shade above him. The elephant is his couch, the birds his pavilion. Do you think no fence surrounds him? Look at the corpses piled around him, human mountains slain by his sword and spear, forming his rampart. White-haired, monkey-bodied old woman, look carefully! See where the vultures settle. Your son lies dead there like a great elephant. He charged like a bull, entered the battlefield, repaid the debt of the red rice in fierce combat, brought down an elephant and became a corpse. That is the son you bore!”

He was her only son, the lamp of the lineage who would plant its memorial stone; she was the grey old mother who bore him. She hastened to the field not hoping he had escaped alive and would return home, but to learn whether he had swept like a storm, torn like a tiger and died gloriously, or become base and shown his back. Amid fallen broad-shouldered warriors she searched in bewilderment until someone showed her that he had whirled like wind through the enemy, killed them, and died in their camp with a spear in his chest.

This picture filled with valour is a brief fragment spoken by the sweet Tamil of the *Purananuru*. It is a ray from the crystal hall of ancient greatness, the record of a victorious lineage now mentally weary, courage forgotten and made futile.

The people of the South, guardians of classical Tamil sweet as fruit and boiled milk, now sing the praise of their enemies. Yet hearing the heroic mode of Tamil could make hair rise and an old man young. Once men feasted in camps where spear was hand, finger was sword, elephant was shield, courage was speech and victory was breath. They attacked and won, or fell with arrows in their ribs and spears in their bodies. Royal mothers did not collapse even at a dead son, but turned over heaps of bodies to see whether the spear had struck his chest or the sword his back. This was the land where Tamil played. Today it flees in terror at a mouse. Descendants of the three crowned kings who beat drums proclaiming themselves tiger-like have broken spines and weakened minds.

Not courage alone but wisdom and love once played in this Tamil garden. Bulls who felled elephants took their beloveds' hands; heroes mounted the palmyra horse to secure marriage. Joy and valour danced together. The inner poems beautifully told how Kurinji, Marutham, Mullai and Neithal rejoiced in lovers' poetic speech, while the outer poems declared courage. The Eight Anthologies were a treasury of delight. That glorious language now stands like a deceived widow who has lost husband, brother and children.

A land of valour now hears weary speech, a homeland of victory drums hears empty shouting. Its warriors fell and the ancient battle stories faded from public memory. Literature, music and drama were Tamil's three children. Literature was sugar candy, music a mine of heart-capturing poetry, and drama enacted thought. Together, Muthamizh was the drum of Tamil warriors. Holes now scar that drum, not from age but from the selfish storm of conspiratorial minds. People with a stepmother's attitude entered while its heroic voice, language of love and path of joy were being plundered.

A Tamil tradition once alive with self-confidence, pride and love of country now bows to anyone who raises a staff. World-rejecting songs promising that worship will remove all suffering, and laments asking God to end one's life, entered the warrior lineage. Poisonous ideas saying “Fate! Everything is his doing; what can I do?” stole self-confidence. A cultural invasion by another people fragmented and tortured Tamil. Deceived by fraudulent teaching, the son of the South lost his mother.

An age that made literature from sunrise, moonlight, a peacock's dance, lightning, secret love, public war, royal elephant armies, women's play and the unconquered Tamil breath gave way to divine antatis, the Rama story, devotional nectar, the Bharata and the *Tiruvilaiyadal Puranam*. Devotees like Kambar and Kilar multiplied. The poet who sang nature began pleading, “Lord and parent, how shall I gain devotion?”

Music celebrating heroic mothers, memorial stones, Chera greatness, Chola halls, Pandya forces, hills, moonlight and lovers sending swans as messengers weakened. Disciples arose to sing of fate, seven births and otherworldly doctrines. Tamil music turned toward temple towers, Krishna's sport with cowherd women and Hanuman's glory. Those who should have sung the mother rejoicing in her son's heroic death now regarded “Muruga, grant me salvation” as music. Tamil fragrance left music and an improper odour entered.

Literature declared this life evil and impermanent and directed people toward the next world. Drama ceased to enact human joy and became dances of Radha and Krishna, stories of Nalayini and Ramayana plays. Muthamizh reached this pitiable state.

The article quotes an order from the Kanchi Kamakoti pontiff asking the Tamil researcher M. Raghava Iyengar, praised for expertise in inscriptions, to contribute monthly to *Arya Dharma*. That, it says, shows the condition to which Tamil research was reduced. Work proceeded behind a screen to erase Tamil's distinct greatness. By giving generously to every newcomer, the Tamil lost his position, forgot himself and the distinction of the Dravidian language, and slept until a serpent could swallow him.

Literature, music and drama slipped away unnoticed. Muthamizh was transformed by doctrines of sin and merit, devotion, heaven and hell.

The South possessed fame and wealth centuries before Christ. Roman history records a political envoy from Tamil country at the court of Augustus Caesar. Egyptians, Persians and Phoenicians traded in Tamil goods. Yet the great rulers who combined valour and wisdom fell, courage weakened, and doctrines that plundered thought entered the Tamil garden.

The historian V. Suryanarayana Sastri is quoted describing a body whose Veda was flesh, Upanishads higher state, Ramayana and Mahabharata lungs, Manusmriti heart, Puranas and Itihasas skeleton, Agamas, Sastras, mantras and rites nerves, religions fat and caste skin. Into Tamil life entered the “divine language.” Gods and humans, high and low, were created; Tamil people and Tamil country declined.

New culture, language and teachings entered. A lineage that sang courage and victory fell to the conspiracy called fate, was bound in the Vedic net and bitten by serpents of sin and merit. The newcomers were presented as holy and capable of granting earthly wealth and heavenly liberation. Devotional intoxication rose and an inferiority complex entered Tamil hearts. Life declined, crowns fell, civilisation and Tamil culture shattered, and the language reached a state that brought tears.

Religious teachers, Saiva saints, Vaishnava Alvars, Buddhist preachers and Jain guardians moved through Tamil Nadu. Tamil was redirected from flourishing life toward turning minds to Mahesvara. The *Tiruvacakam*, *Tiruppavai*, Rama story and similar works flourished through religious rivalry, but made the warrior a coward rather than a bull of battle. Muthamizh was broken into devotional music, superstition-spreading literature and orthodox drama. Alien ideas grew as trees in the Tamil grove. The fall of the three crowned dynasties, foreign invasions and outsiders' entry accelerated it. In the land of Nedunchezhiyan, Karikannanar, Kadumperungo, Senguttuvan, Ilanaganar, Kavarpendu and Vellaippadini, names such as Arumugan, Subramanian, Govindan, Narayanasami, Ranganathan, Jagadambal and Nilayadakshi became common. Even people's and places' names changed.

Yet every country has known renaissance. Old practices change and new energy appears. Such an awakening now enters the South: a determination to restore Tamil greatness, defend the mother tongue and demolish manipulation. The anti-Hindi struggle and the graves of Thalamuthu and Natarasan proclaim the resolve to wage a moral battle and die for Tamil. Tamil youth have become spear points forged in a furnace. Scholars once content to sing old greatness now walk smiling, hand in hand, on a new road.

There is new flowering in literature, uprising in music and ferment in drama. Even magazines accustomed to an “ammami” style must carry the new age's writing. Readers favour and respect a transformed Tamil prose. New books and journals bloom. Scholars who once recoiled from rationalism now become students of renaissance.

This new condition appeared proudly at the Muthamizh Development Conference held in Coimbatore on the 27th and 28th of the previous month. Tamil scholars, thousands of young defenders, countless courageous women and students, sculptors of the future, assembled. Coimbatore, cradle of the Kongu country, resounded with Tamil through every street for two days. The comrades who organised the conference and devised its programme created an image of Dravidian resurgence. Despite exhaustion and worry, they served with smiles and won distinction for themselves and the country.

They were poor but not cowardly, without millionaire support, fashionable elites, brocade turbans or former ministers. The conference seemed to declare: “We are workers from the dust, but we are the people.”

Lenin said art must contain the poor person's tears, the worker's suffering, the labourer's heartbeat and the people's mind. Art is not the genteel conversation of the wealthy, a pastime of luxury or a means of passing leisure. It must belong to the people and straighten their bent lives, not remain an unapproachable flame but shine like the growing moon. The conference seemed to fulfil that ideal.

Those learned in Muthamizh gathered, and literature, music and drama flourished. The event was not convened by opportunists seeking titles and offices, seasonal merchants asking what commodity would sell, or selfish people seeking to fertilise their own gardens with the public's love of Tamil. It was assembled by the Dravidian movement that had raised the courageous cry that batons and bullets would not cause fear or fatigue.

The gathering included Navalar Bharathiyar, the vigorous scholar and “treasury of classical Tamil”; Bharathidasan, lion-hearted poet of Dravidian awakening; Avvai Duraisami; Vellai Varananar; multilingual scholar Appaduraiyar; Rama Meenakshisundaranar; Sundararasan; Self-Respect campaigner Ponnambalanar; and Annal Thango, whose pure-Tamil zeal transformed Sanskritic personal names into Tamil ones.

Musicians Chidambaram Jayaram, Tiruvarur Namasivayam and Rajamanicka Bhagavatar demonstrated their love of Muthamizh. N. S. Krishnan presented the history of Valluvar through *villu pattu*, spreading good ideas through humour. K. R. Ramasami sang Udumalai Narayana Kavi's conference greeting in his resonant voice. Film and stage actors, producers, playwrights, screenwriters, musicians, writers, editors and poets from all three fields participated.

To encourage new-age poets, the conference held a poetry contest. Bharathidasan and Appaduraiyar judged entries on “The Smile of Beauty” and awarded first and second prizes to Mudiyarasu and Vanidasan. More than forty young poets participated, demonstrating readiness to defend Tamil greatness.

For two days the movement's leading figures rejoiced that such an event had become a popular festival. They felt Tamil would flourish and arrogance would end. Though organised quickly, the Coimbatore conference became the victory cry of a fallen society. It unanimously adopted measures to cultivate and protect Tamil, most notably establishing the Muthamizh Perumandram.

A new age has begun in the South. Defenders of language and country are preparing. The cry “Tamil! Tamil!” arises not within one party but wherever Tamil people stand across all parties. This condition must grow, yet obstacles and storms now facing it trouble the heart.

As a fallen society struggles upright, a hammer strikes its head: Hindi, unnecessary to this country, is forcibly imposed. One alien linguistic invasion has already damaged the mother tongue. At the very moment Tamil youth declare they will become a volcano against every plot, another language is imposed through northern domination and the power of another culture holding office.

No one willingly accepts destruction or conducts a welcome for death. Hindi is again being introduced into the Tamil garden and must be resisted. The mother tongue must not only be defended but methodically developed. That desire created the Muthamizh Perumandram. With Navalar Bharathiyar as president and Bharathidasan as secretary, its future work needs little explanation.

Muthamizh is already rising. Epics such as *Ravana Kaviyam* and new scientific books are appearing in literature. The Tamil music movement increasingly sounds the call not to sing degrading ideas and destroy reason. Puranic drama gives way daily to social reform plays and new works spreading rational thought.

Muthamizh has begun to grow. Yet the OCR-damaged final comparison says a ruler once possessed power to obstruct such growth, and likens that condition to the Nehru government's course. While people cry in hunger and helplessness, Nehru travels as the “Light of Asia,” sending friends and favourites throughout the world as envoys. People did not expect these painful contradictions under “their” government, yet Nehru is becoming Nero.

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 11 June 1950.

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim. The title is visibly damaged as “புலி நிகர் மாத்தரே?7”; the English follows the likely sense “Are They Men Equal to Tigers?” without correcting the Tamil heading. Several lines at page transitions, especially near the end, are incomplete or reordered by OCR; the translation follows the recoverable argument and marks the final comparison as damaged. Historical names and titles are transliterated conservatively. The article's account of Tamil cultural change and its polemical descriptions of religious and linguistic influence are preserved as the author's historical viewpoint, not independently endorsed factual conclusions. Quoted literary lines are translated by sense rather than treated as critical editions.
